This file is a list of compiler/library issues that affect CORE-MATH
correctness or efficiency.

Correctness issues
==================

Open issues:

https://gcc.gnu.org/bugzilla/show_bug.cgi?id=106902
Program compiled with -O3 -mfma produces different result

https://gcc.gnu.org/bugzilla/show_bug.cgi?id=34678
#pragma STDC FENV_ACCESS ON is not supported by GCC

Fixed issues (for the record):

GCC <= 13 wrongly converts some BF16 constants:
https://gcc.gnu.org/bugzilla/show_bug.cgi?id=111867
aarch64: Wrong code for bf16 literal load when the arch support +fp16

https://gcc.gnu.org/bugzilla/show_bug.cgi?id=57245
Floating-point constant truncation ignores -frounding-math

The Apple ldexp() function had a bug in the subnormal range (Darwin 25.1.0).
For example ldexp(0x1.9192d74f53405p-6, -1027) gave 0x1.9192d74f53p-1033
instead of 0x1.9192d74f538p-1033. This was reported at FB21774989 and FB21774410,
and fixed in latest beta of macOS Tahoe 26.4 (build 25E5218f).
This was found by JohnMather from a failure in CORE-MATH erfc.

Efficiency issues
=================

https://gcc.gnu.org/bugzilla/show_bug.cgi?id=127870
Reassociation of __int128 sum costs a callee-saved register on x86-64
This mainly affects the efficiency of some binary128 functions, where gcc
is slower than clang:
$ CORE_MATH_SIMPLE_STAT=true PERF_REPEAT=50 ./perf.sh tanq # gcc 16.2.0
342.407
$ CC=clang CORE_MATH_SIMPLE_STAT=true PERF_REPEAT=50 ./perf.sh tanq # clang 21.1.8
279.652

